// THE LAYOUT MIGRATION GENERATOR (gunbc.plans.source_layout_authority, C0/C1). It is a .dag
// instrument, not a throwaway script: the sweep is a construction input derived from the tree it
// runs on, so it is re-runnable on whatever base the branch is merged onto and idempotent once the
// tree is at the rule's positions (a placed tree plans no change).
//
// This module owns the effects (walk, read, write); the placement and the collision refusals are
// layout_migration_map, which is pure.

// Decisions for the references the tables cannot decide, one row per distinct reference, each with
// its reason (see RunDisposition). The census reports a reference with no row as undecided and a row
// no reference uses as unused; the sweep refuses to run on either. A placed tree plans no change, so
// the table is empty here: a re-run on a base that has gained a namespace which vanishes into more
// than one parent adds a row for it, and the row leaves with the sweep that used it.
data layout_dispositions: List<RunDisposition> = []

// Values that spell a module name without referring to the module (see IdentityLiteral): wire
// audiences and issuers a deployed peer compares byte for byte, and the markers a content hash or a
// signature is derived from, whose rename would change every derived value with no version bump.
// Each is pinned in its file by the path the file has after the sweep and the exact text it keeps.
// The texts are built from their segments so that this table spells no module name: the sweep
// reads this file too, and a table the sweep could rewrite would pin the wrong text on the next run.
fn spelled(segments: List<String>) -> String {
  join(segments, ".")
}

fn device_redemption_audience() -> String {
  concat(spelled(segments: ["gunbc", "roadmap_serve"]), "/device-redemption")
}

data layout_identity_literals: List<IdentityLiteral> = [
